Subject: [PATCH] arm64: limit the TB371FC early page-table pool to its board

The exported handoff replaces every early page-table allocation with a
256-page static pool intended for the TB371FC vendor-kernel kexec path.
Booting the resulting research kernel on QEMU virt with 1 GiB RAM and
the normal rodata policy panics in map_mem() when that pool is exhausted.

Check the flattened root compatible, which is available before the live
device tree is unflattened. Keep the existing pool and its exhaustion
check for lenovo,tb371fc. For other machines, including an ACPI boot with
no flattened tree, restore the upstream memblock allocation and failure
check. Do not increase the pool or relax read-only memory protections to
make the VM pass.

This contains a board-specific workaround; it does not resolve the
original tablet handoff/DMA problem or validate the pool on this owner's
tablet. Apply after the Linux 6.12.114 handoff working-tree patch.

diff --git a/arch/arm64/mm/mmu.c b/arch/arm64/mm/mmu.c
--- a/arch/arm64/mm/mmu.c
+++ b/arch/arm64/mm/mmu.c
@@ -24,6 +24,7 @@
 #include <linux/vmalloc.h>
 #include <linux/set_memory.h>
 #include <linux/kfence.h>
+#include <linux/of_fdt.h>
 #include <linux/pkeys.h>
 
 #include <asm/barrier.h>
@@ -117,7 +118,22 @@
 static phys_addr_t __init early_pgtable_alloc(int shift)
 {
 	void *page;
+	phys_addr_t phys;
+
+	/*
+	 * The vendor-kernel handoff workaround is specific to this board.
+	 * The live OF tree is not available at this early boot stage.
+	 */
+	if (!initial_boot_params ||
+	    !of_flat_dt_is_compatible(of_get_flat_dt_root(), "lenovo,tb371fc")) {
+		phys = memblock_phys_alloc_range(PAGE_SIZE, PAGE_SIZE, 0,
+						 MEMBLOCK_ALLOC_NOLEAKTRACE);
+		if (!phys)
+			panic("Failed to allocate page table page\n");
+
+		return phys;
+	}
 
 	if (tb371fc_early_pgtable_pool_used >=
 	    TB371FC_EARLY_PGTABLE_POOL_PAGES)
 		panic("TB371FC embedded early page-table pool exhausted\n");
